# Abstract identities for the LWS 1.0 test definitions (YAML-LD).
#
# Tests never carry credentials. They name an identity -- `as: alice` for the
# Authorization header of a request, `${credential.didkey}` for a subject token in a
# token-exchange body -- and the harness resolves the name against this registry.
# EXECUTION.md section 5 is the contract for how each kind is produced.
"@context":
  - ./context.jsonld
  - ./touchstone.jsonld
id: "#identities"
type: IdentityRegistry
label: Identities used by the LWS 1.0 test definitions
comment: >-
  Three kinds. NoCredential sends nothing. StorageAccessToken identities act on the
  storage server through `as:` and send `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`.
  SubjectCredential identities never appear in `as:`; their credential is the
  subject_token a test presents at the authorization server's token endpoint. A fault
  identity is its basis identity with exactly one defect (the fault terms are defined
  in vocab.yamlld), so a failing test names a single cause.
identities:

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- storage identities

  - id: "#anonymous"
    type: Identity
    label: No credentials
    kind: NoCredential
    comment: Requests carry no Authorization header.

  - id: "#alice"
    type: Identity
    label: Storage owner
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    comment: >-
      The agent that controls the target's storage. The harness provisions ${run.root}
      and every ${test.container} as alice, and a step without `as:` runs as alice. Her
      access token comes from the target configuration, in order of preference: minted
      by the harness when the target declares HarnessIssuedTokens; obtained by exchanging
      a configured subject credential at the storage's authorization server; or a static
      bearer token from TOUCHSTONE_TOKEN_ALICE. On a target that does not enforce
      authentication alice sends no Authorization header. ${identity.alice.webid} is the
      agent IRI named in the target configuration.

  - id: "#bob"
    type: Identity
    label: Another authenticated agent
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    comment: >-
      An agent the storage authenticates but that holds no permission on alice's
      resources unless a test grants one. Credential sources as for alice, with
      TOUCHSTONE_TOKEN_BOB as the static fallback. ${identity.bob.webid} comes from the
      target configuration.

  # ------------------------------------------- access-token faults (storage validation)
  #
  # Derivable from a real token (no signing key needed): SignatureCorrupted, AlgNone,
  # UnknownKeyId. Everything else is an otherwise-valid token and needs the harness to
  # hold the authorization server's signing key (HarnessIssuedTokens); a real server's
  # AS will not sign a broken token on request.

  - id: "#alice-expired"
    type: Identity
    label: alice with an expired access token
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: Expired
    comment: >-
      Minted when HarnessIssuedTokens is declared. Otherwise derived by holding a real
      alice token until its exp has passed plus 60 s of clock skew, when that exp is no
      more than 600 s away; if neither is possible the identity is unavailable and tests
      using it are inapplicable.

  - id: "#alice-bad-signature"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token with a corrupted signature
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: SignatureCorrupted

  - id: "#alice-alg-none"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token re-encoded unsigned (alg none)
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: AlgNone

  - id: "#alice-unknown-key"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token re-signed by a key the storage cannot know
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: UnknownKeyId

  - id: "#alice-wrong-audience"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token minted for a different storage
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: WrongAudience
    requires: [HarnessIssuedTokens]

  - id: "#alice-wrong-issuer"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token claiming an untrusted issuer
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: WrongIssuer
    requires: [HarnessIssuedTokens]

  - id: "#alice-not-yet-valid"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token whose nbf lies an hour ahead
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: NotYetValid
    requires: [HarnessIssuedTokens]

  - id: "#alice-issued-in-future"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token whose iat lies an hour ahead
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: IssuedInFuture
    requires: [HarnessIssuedTokens]

  - id: "#alice-multiple-audiences"
    type: Identity
    label: alice's access token naming two audiences
    kind: StorageAccessToken
    basis: alice
    fault: MultipleAudiences
    requires: [HarnessIssuedTokens]

  # ------------------------- self-issued credentials for a DID subject (did:key)

  - id: "#didkey"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential for a did:key subject
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    comment: >-
      A controlled-identifier credential whose subject is a DID (CID suite section 1). A
      P-256 key pair generated fresh for each run. ${identity.didkey.webid} is its did:key
      identifier (multicodec p256-pub, compressed point, base58btc: did:key:zDn...), and
      ${self.webid} inside the templates below is the same value. ${self.kid} is the
      identifier's multibase value, so the kid names the one verification method,
      ${self.webid}#${self.kid}, of the DID document the did:key method derives. It has no
      webid template and nothing is hosted: the verifier resolves the identifier itself,
      so no trust needs setting up and the target need not reach the harness.
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"

  - id: "#didkey-bad-signature"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential with a corrupted signature
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: SignatureCorrupted

  - id: "#didkey-alg-none"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential re-encoded unsigned (alg none)
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: AlgNone

  - id: "#didkey-expired"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential that expired an hour ago
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: Expired

  - id: "#didkey-claims-mismatch"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential whose client_id differs from sub and iss
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: ClientIdMismatch

  - id: "#didkey-audience-excludes-as"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential whose aud does not include the authorization server
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: AudienceExcludesAuthorizationServer

  - id: "#didkey-missing-exp"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential without an exp claim
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: MissingExpiration

  - id: "#didkey-missing-iat"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential without an iat claim
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: didkey
    fault: MissingIssuedAt

  - id: "#didkey-missing-sub"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential without a sub claim
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    comment: >-
      The did:key credential with no sub claim, otherwise intact and signed with the
      run's did:key key. The subject claim is REQUIRED (core 7.1). No fault term
      expresses a missing claim of the core data model, and the fault vocabulary is part
      of the frozen format, so the claims are written out (D-0062).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"

  - id: "#didkey-missing-iss"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential without an iss claim
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    comment: >-
      The did:key credential with no iss claim, otherwise intact and signed with the
      run's did:key key. The issuer claim is REQUIRED (core 7.1). No fault term
      expresses a missing claim of the core data model, and the fault vocabulary is part
      of the frozen format, so the claims are written out (D-0062).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"

  - id: "#didkey-missing-client-id"
    type: Identity
    label: did:key credential without a client_id claim
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    comment: >-
      The did:key credential with no client_id claim, otherwise intact and signed with the
      run's did:key key. The client claim is REQUIRED (core 7.1). No fault term
      expresses a missing claim of the core data model, and the fault vocabulary is part
      of the frozen format, so the claims are written out (D-0062).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"

  # ---------------------------------------------------- OpenID Connect ID Tokens

  - id: "#oidc"
    type: Identity
    label: ID Token from the harness OpenID Provider
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-openid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:id_token
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/oidc"
    comment: >-
      The harness runs an OpenID Provider at ${fixtures.baseUrl}op (OpenID Connect
      Discovery at ${fixtures.baseUrl}op/.well-known/openid-configuration, JWKS at the
      jwks_uri it names) and hosts the subject's controlled identifier document at
      ${identity.oidc.webid}. A verifier with no prior trust in that OP must dereference
      sub, find the OpenIdProvider service equal to iss, and discover the signing key
      (OIDC suite section 5). The target must be able to reach ${fixtures.baseUrl}.
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${fixtures.baseUrl}op"
      azp: "${fixtures.baseUrl}clients/touchstone"
      aud: ["${fixtures.baseUrl}clients/touchstone", "${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${self.webid}"
      service:
        - type: https://www.w3.org/ns/lws#OpenIdProvider
          serviceEndpoint: "${fixtures.baseUrl}op"

  - id: "#oidc-expired"
    type: Identity
    label: ID Token that expired an hour ago
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: oidc
    fault: Expired

  - id: "#oidc-alg-none"
    type: Identity
    label: ID Token re-encoded unsigned (alg none)
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: oidc
    fault: AlgNone

  - id: "#oidc-bad-signature"
    type: Identity
    label: ID Token with a corrupted signature
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: oidc
    fault: SignatureCorrupted

  - id: "#oidc-untrusted-issuer"
    type: Identity
    label: ID Token from an OpenID Provider the subject never named
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: oidc
    fault: UntrustedIssuer

  - id: "#oidc-missing-azp"
    type: Identity
    label: ID Token without the azp (client identifier) claim
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: oidc
    fault: MissingAuthorizedParty

  # ------------------------------------ self-issued controlled-identifier credentials

  - id: "#cid"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential for a harness-hosted controlled identifier
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/${run.id}/cid"
    comment: >-
      A P-256 key pair generated fresh for each run, published as a JsonWebKey
      verification method in the controlled identifier document the harness hosts at
      ${identity.cid.webid}. The JWT header kid names that method (CID suite section 5,
      CID 1.0 section 3.3). The target must be able to reach ${fixtures.baseUrl}. The
      document's URL names the run, as do those of the other CID identities, which carry the
      same key: a verifier may cache a document, and an earlier run's copy names another key
      (D-0091).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${self.webid}"
      authentication:
        - id: "${self.webid}#${self.kid}"
          type: JsonWebKey
          controller: "${self.webid}"
          publicKeyJwk: "${self.publicJwk}"

  - id: "#cid-document-id-mismatch"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose controlled identifier document names another id
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/${run.id}/cid-document-id-mismatch"
    comment: >-
      The document at the subject URL gives a different id, so it is not the subject's document. The credential itself is well formed and signed with the run's CID key; only the
      document the harness hosts differs from #cid's (D-0074).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/someone-else"
      authentication:
        - id: "${self.webid}#${self.kid}"
          type: JsonWebKey
          controller: "${self.webid}"
          publicKeyJwk: "${self.publicJwk}"
  - id: "#cid-key-not-for-authentication"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose key the document lists, but not for authentication
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/${run.id}/cid-key-not-for-authentication"
    comment: >-
      The key is a verificationMethod referenced only from assertionMethod, so the subject does not authenticate with it (CID 1.0 section 3.3). The credential itself is well formed and signed with the run's CID key; only the
      document the harness hosts differs from #cid's (D-0074).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${self.webid}"
      verificationMethod:
        - id: "${self.webid}#${self.kid}"
          type: JsonWebKey
          controller: "${self.webid}"
          publicKeyJwk: "${self.publicJwk}"
      assertionMethod: ["${self.webid}#${self.kid}"]
  - id: "#cid-key-revoked"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose verification method has been revoked
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/${run.id}/cid-key-revoked"
    comment: >-
      The authentication method carries a revoked time in the past (CID 1.0 section 2.2). The credential itself is well formed and signed with the run's CID key; only the
      document the harness hosts differs from #cid's (D-0074).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${self.webid}"
      authentication:
        - id: "${self.webid}#${self.kid}"
          type: JsonWebKey
          controller: "${self.webid}"
          publicKeyJwk: "${self.publicJwk}"
          revoked: "2000-01-01T00:00:00Z"
  - id: "#cid-foreign-controller"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose verification method another identifier controls
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/${run.id}/cid-foreign-controller"
    comment: >-
      The authentication method names another document as its controller, so it is not the subject's key. The credential itself is well formed and signed with the run's CID key; only the
      document the harness hosts differs from #cid's (D-0074).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${self.webid}"
      authentication:
        - id: "${self.webid}#${self.kid}"
          type: JsonWebKey
          controller: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/someone-else"
          publicKeyJwk: "${self.publicJwk}"
  - id: "#cid-referenced-method"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose authentication method is referenced, not embedded
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-ssi-cid/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
    algorithm: ES256
    requires: [ReachableFixtures]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/${run.id}/cid-referenced-method"
    comment: >-
      The authentication relationship names the method by its id, and the method is defined under verificationMethod: the reference form CID 1.0 allows beside embedding. The credential itself is well formed and signed with the run's CID key; only the
      document the harness hosts differs from #cid's (D-0074).
    credentialHeader:
      alg: ES256
      typ: JWT
      kid: "${self.kid}"
    credentialClaims:
      sub: "${self.webid}"
      iss: "${self.webid}"
      client_id: "${self.webid}"
      aud: ["${as.issuer}"]
      iat: "${now}"
      exp: "${now+300}"
    identityDocument:
      "@context": ["https://www.w3.org/ns/cid/v1"]
      id: "${self.webid}"
      verificationMethod:
        - id: "${self.webid}#${self.kid}"
          type: JsonWebKey
          controller: "${self.webid}"
          publicKeyJwk: "${self.publicJwk}"
      authentication: ["${self.webid}#${self.kid}"]
  - id: "#cid-unknown-kid"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose kid names no verification method
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: cid
    fault: UnknownKeyId

  - id: "#cid-bad-signature"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential with a corrupted signature
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: cid
    fault: SignatureCorrupted

  - id: "#cid-expired"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential that expired an hour ago
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: cid
    fault: Expired

  - id: "#cid-alg-none"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential re-encoded unsigned (alg none)
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: cid
    fault: AlgNone

  - id: "#cid-claims-mismatch"
    type: Identity
    label: Self-issued credential whose client_id differs from sub and iss
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: cid
    fault: ClientIdMismatch

  # ------------------------------------------------------------- SAML 2.0 assertions

  - id: "#saml"
    type: Identity
    label: SAML 2.0 assertion from the harness identity provider
    kind: SubjectCredential
    suite: https://www.w3.org/TR/lws10-authn-saml/
    tokenType: urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:saml2
    algorithm: http://www.w3.org/2001/04/xmldsig-more#rsa-sha256
    requires: [SamlTrust]
    webid: "${fixtures.baseUrl}agents/saml"
    comment: >-
      A signed saml:Assertion from the harness IdP (entity ${fixtures.baseUrl}idp),
      presented base64url-encoded as RFC 8693 section 3 requires. The SAML suite leaves
      trust to out-of-band configuration, so the target must already trust the harness
      IdP's signing certificate (SamlTrust). Instants are xsd:dateTime in UTC.
    samlAssertion:
      issuer: "${fixtures.baseUrl}idp"
      nameId: "${self.webid}"
      nameIdFormat: urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:2.0:nameid-format:persistent
      recipient: "${fixtures.baseUrl}clients/touchstone"
      audience: ["${fixtures.baseUrl}clients/touchstone", "${as.issuer}"]
      notBefore: "${now}"
      notOnOrAfter: "${now+300}"

  - id: "#saml-bad-signature"
    type: Identity
    label: SAML assertion altered after signing
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: saml
    fault: SignatureCorrupted

  - id: "#saml-unsigned"
    type: Identity
    label: SAML assertion without a signature
    kind: SubjectCredential
    basis: saml
    fault: Unsigned
